:answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/50/503598e9174ef0276190165b397e5e9d69972e0df9ac6e83a9e4f9258b98fb60.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bebf4bb7-c750-5197-a195-cb242332a065" :answer/id #uuid "bebf4bb7-c750-5197-a195-cb242332a065" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/a4/a450448e2275dd606de81f8fc2f3ed2bc46ec0e0a13afc0ddfa4b043e1d4f0c2.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-520ed5d8-6c46-5467-ac97-6eaeca88d2f4" :answer/id #uuid "520ed5d8-6c46-5467-ac97-6eaeca88d2f4" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/f1/f160dffa224f97f932636137b2559ebee650ff6be0853206b3b0a89ed2bfab98.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186074420 :question/problem "![](images/7d/7d2474d5fd25cbb23c534e4d8f67726177988a49f9c0e271b91b0ff677dabd41.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate $\\frac{2}{3}\\times \\frac{1}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we need to multiply by $\\frac{1}{4}$, we split the whole into $4$ equal parts horizontally.\n\n![](images/41/416c66bc1591d9215bd5e74c975f6e17992ae0d3456262091d48910b9d89abc9.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents $\\frac{2}{12}$ of the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}\\times \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{2}{12}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186072758 :answer-field/correct 17592186072759 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-8fb4c868-ce54-5c7d-ab79-c96f1e73ab38" :question/id #uuid "8fb4c868-ce54-5c7d-ab79-c96f1e73ab38" :question/math-academy-id "q-60226" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e8213704b9b6ba8e507ec59a09d5e6fe1450e21fa3e1f26eca15876a5807fdc.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate $\\frac{2}{4}\\times \\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we need to multiply by $\\frac{1}{3}$, we split the whole into $3$ equal parts horizontally.\n\n![](images/37/37d524644c009517608455822101750eb532cc8858fee1b96516d30e3fa1fe15.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents $\\frac{2}{12}$ of the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{4}\\times \\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{2}{12}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9bf9904d-ff91-59ca-838c-990a934ab97e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9bf9904d-ff91-59ca-838c-990a934ab97e" :answer-field/id #uuid "9bf9904d-ff91-59ca-838c-990a934ab97e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b3ccd621-328f-52d1-b0a5-229fe95a49be" :answer-field/choices ["answer-15321b92-66dd-5be5-aeba-0321bfb68de6" "answer-a1f76a54-7b84-55e5-85d6-2f2641897d2c" "answer-b3ccd621-328f-52d1-b0a5-229fe95a49be" "answer-e388c151-72a9-5f49-b673-7acdc8cfea3b" "answer-b6a17329-a593-5b33-9648-7fdc0bcc4009"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15321b92-66dd-5be5-aeba-0321bfb68de6" :answer/id #uuid "15321b92-66dd-5be5-aeba-0321bfb68de6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1f76a54-7b84-55e5-85d6-2f2641897d2c" :answer/id #uuid "a1f76a54-7b84-55e5-85d6-2f2641897d2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3ccd621-328f-52d1-b0a5-229fe95a49be" :answer/id #uuid "b3ccd621-328f-52d1-b0a5-229fe95a49be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e388c151-72a9-5f49-b673-7acdc8cfea3b" :answer/id #uuid "e388c151-72a9-5f49-b673-7acdc8cfea3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6a17329-a593-5b33-9648-7fdc0bcc4009" :answer/id #uuid "b6a17329-a593-5b33-9648-7fdc0bcc4009" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186075055 :tutorial/content "We can use fraction models to understand how to multiply fractions.\n\nFor example, let's suppose that we wish to compute the following product of fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3} \\times \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nLet's start by representing $\\dfrac23$ using a square fraction model, like the one below. Notice that we have split the fraction *vertically*.\n\n![](images/dc/dc72f1bc6250e2ca39cf424847d3e592858d8cd6765cade9d357a139bbd906b0.png)\n\nSince we need to multiply the above fraction by $\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}2}$, we split the whole into $\\color{blue}2$ equal parts *horizontally*. This way, each of the original pieces is split in half. Then, we only keep the shaded pieces in the bottom row.\n\n![](images/41/41d47349763b17ed06777b732997ab4f229088b38252c398854a92a1d62ccb6a.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we see that the shaded part represents $\\dfrac{2}{6}$ of the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3} \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{2}{6}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can simplify this answer further by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2.$ However, in this lesson, we'll focus just on multiplication and not worry about simplifying the final answers." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073983 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
